\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{From move sequences to play}

\p{A formal move description becomes useful only when a player can apply it
and reproduce its result. Play gives the mathematics an immediate test of
intelligibility: a notation should identify a move unambiguously, a
displayed turn should agree with the stated action, and replaying a
sequence should reproduce the same state. Scrambling, undoing, recognizing
a case, and following a solution all depend on those agreements.}

\subtree{
  \title{A replay contract}

  \p{Let #{S} be a declared state space and let every legal move #{m} be a
  reversible map #{S\to S}. A notation parser is correct only if it maps a
  token to one such map, with an inverse token satisfying}
  ##{m^{-1}m=\operatorname{id}_S.}
  \p{For a word #{w=m_1m_2\cdots m_k}, we read moves from left to right as
  in [the complete layer-first method](frap-0017):}
  ##{s_0=s,\qquad s_i=m_i(s_{i-1}),\qquad
      \operatorname{replay}(w,s)=s_k.}
  \p{A replay test therefore records the initial state, the token list, the
  convention for viewing each face, and the final state. Replaying #{w}
  followed by #{w^{-1}} must return to #{s}; two displays are equivalent
  only after their resulting states, rather than their typography, agree.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Two traces that expose the conventions}

  \p{The [tetrahedron turn](frap-000O) gives a short noncommuting trace.
  Starting at the recorded incidence position #{(1,124)}, the #{H}-move
  followed by the #{K}-move reaches #{(4,134)}. Reversing the order reaches
  #{(3,234)}. The two words are therefore different maps, even though each
  move separately has order three. A player can check this by stepping
  through the two token lists and comparing the final records.}

  \p{The [cube face turn](frap-000P) fixes the frame and turns only the
  chosen layer. Its coordinate map #{q(x,y,z)=(y,-x,z)} sends the four
  #{z=1} corners around a four-cycle while leaving the opposite layer
  fixed. This is a useful display test: a global rotation of the drawing is
  not the same operation as the local puzzle move, and the unturned center
  must not move merely because the picture was rotated.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{From recognition to a solve}

  \p{A reproducible solve has five recorded stages: identify the state in
  the chosen coordinates; state the target condition; choose a move word;
  replay it from the recorded state; and check the target and preserved
  invariants. The [layer-first method](frap-0017) supplies a human version:
  each stage fixes information and uses a stabilizer for what remains. The
  [computer account](frap-0018) supplies a search version: a returned word
  is an upper bound, while a projected-distance table gives a lower bound.}

  \p{These certificates should be reported separately. A valid replay shows
  that the word reaches the target. A short word improves an upper bound in
  the named metric. A shortest-path claim additionally needs an exhaustive
  search or a matching lower bound. The distinction also applies to a
  scramble: [scrambling and spectra](frap-0015) requires the move law and
  length before a distributional claim can be evaluated.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{What a playing interface can check}

  \p{A small interface can make the contract visible without pretending to
  replace it. It can reject an unknown token, show the affected pieces,
  step through a word, undo by inverse tokens, and compare the final state
  with a target. It can also display parity, orientation, or subgroup
  coordinates as diagnostics, provided each coordinate is tied to the
  declared state model.}

  \p{An animation illustrates a claim; the move map and replay log are the
  evidence for it. The interface should therefore expose the convention,
  the exact token sequence, and the before-and-after state so another
  person or program can reproduce the check. The existing
  [Rubik's Cube resources](uts-016J) collect notation, methods, and
  demonstrations; the companion [FRAP repository](https://github.com/utensil/frap)
  can later host executable versions of these checks.}
}
